<!--
  navigate_to_pose_tricycle_v3.xml - m5-ver3's navigate-to-pose tree.
  F4 Task 2. Read only by `m5v3.sh start --nav`, through bt_navigator's
  `default_nav_to_pose_bt_xml`, which config.yaml owns (nav.bt_xml) and
  m5v3.sh passes as an absolute path.

  IT IS DERIVED FROM agv/forklift/behavior_trees/
  navigate_to_pose_tricycle.xml AND THAT FILE IS READ-ONLY TO THIS TRACK
  (CONTEXT.md). What is taken is the ARGUMENT - which recoveries a
  vehicle that cannot rotate in place may have - because that argument
  is about a tricycle rather than about a floor, and this vehicle is the
  same kinematic machine. What is NOT taken is the crib's third
  selector: it carries a GoalCheckerSelector for a staged docking
  approach, and this track has ONE goal checker and no docking claim
  (nav2.yaml, controller_server). A selector with nothing to select
  between is a moving part that can only go wrong.

  ---- WHY THERE IS A TREE HERE AT ALL ----

  nav2's default tree, nav2_bt_navigator/behavior_trees/
  navigate_to_pose_w_replanning_and_recovery.xml, ends its recovery
  RoundRobin with

      <Spin spin_dist="1.57" .../>
      <Wait wait_duration="5.0"/>
      <BackUp backup_dist="0.30" backup_speed="0.15" .../>

  and both of the motion recoveries assume a base this vehicle is not.

  SPIN IS IMPOSSIBLE HERE AND THE REFUSAL IS MEASURED. It commands
  angular.z with linear.x = 0. One steered wheel a wheelbase ahead of
  the rear axle cannot turn the body without travelling, so there is no
  (steer, tread) pair that produces that twist -
  nodes/cmd_vel_tricycle_core.twist_to_tricycle REFUSES it by name and
  counts the refusal on /m5v3/navcmd/status. With Spin left in the tree
  the vehicle would stand still for the whole behaviour and the
  behaviour would then report SUCCESS. The counter on that status topic
  is how EVIDENCE_NAV_V3.md proves this tree never asked.

  BACKUP IS POSSIBLE AND IS STILL REMOVED, which is the less obvious
  half. nav2_behaviors' DriveOnHeading publishes linear.x with
  angular.z = 0 - a straight line, which this vehicle drives in both
  directions. It is out for three reasons, none of them kinematic:

    - Its collision check simulates a STRAIGHT line from the current
      pose. The steer axis is wherever the controller last left it and
      takes up to 0.625 s to slew back to centre (1.25 rad at
      model.sdf's 2.0 rad/s, and EVIDENCE_NAV_V3.md 5 measured the
      terminal carrying exactly that ramp), so the vehicle drives an ARC
      through space the check never examined.
    - 0.30 m is 11 % of this vehicle's 2.735 m length. Sized to actually
      change the situation it would be metres, and a blind multi-metre
      move with 1.875 m of fork leading is not something a machine this
      size should do because a plan failed.
    - Reverse on this vehicle is not a recovery, it is the ORDINARY
      DIRECTION OF TRAVEL: the forks are at model -x and every profile
      this track has driven commands a negative linear.x (nav2.yaml
      section D). The Reeds-Shepp planner emits it against the map
      whenever it is the right answer. A blind reverse recovery
      duplicates the planner without the map, which is strictly worse.

  WHAT IS LEFT, AND IT IS THE POINT RATHER THAN A LOSS: clear the
  costmaps, wait, replan - and if it still cannot plan, STOP AND REPORT.
  A planner that refuses is better than one that silently fails or
  routes through a rack, and a tree with no motion recovery is what
  makes the refusal prompt and quiet instead of a machine milling about
  in an aisle.

  ---- WHAT IS STOCK, DELIBERATELY ----

  The 1 Hz replan rate, the six top-level retries, the two per-branch
  costmap clears, the ControllerSelector/PlannerSelector pair and the
  two error codes are nav2's own and are not re-argued here. The
  selectors have nothing publishing to them on this stack, so each falls
  back to its default - `FollowPath` and `GridBased`, which are the only
  plugins nav2.yaml declares - and the tree behaves as though they were
  not there. They are kept because they are how a later task adds a
  second controller without editing this file.

  ---- THE ONE NODE THAT IS OURS: DirectionStablePath, G5 TASK 5 ----

  AMR-DEC-004, and it is the whole of the fix G5's experiments selected.
  m5_ver3/bt_direction_stable/src/direction_stable_path.cpp carries the
  argument; what belongs here is where it sits and why exactly there.

  IT WRAPS THE ComputePathToPose RecoveryNode, INSIDE THE RateController.
  Inside, because it must see one plan per REPLAN and not one per BT tick
  - outside, it would be ticked at bt_loop_duration (10 ms) and compare a
  fresh plan against a plan from 10 ms ago. Wrapping the RecoveryNode
  rather than the ComputePathToPose action, because the recovery branch
  (WouldAPlannerRecoveryHelp + ClearEntireCostmap) is how a failed plan
  is retried and that structure is not this task's to change: what
  reaches this decorator is whatever the recovery finally SUCCEEDED with.

  WHAT IT DOES, IN ONE LINE: on child SUCCESS, if the fresh plan's
  first-segment driving direction is opposite to the direction of the
  segment of the ACCEPTED plan the truck is now standing on, for the SAME
  goal, while |v| > hold_speed, the accepted plan is written back to
  {path}. Nothing else.
    THE SEGMENT THE TRUCK IS ON, AND NOT THE ONE THE PLAN SET OFF ON.
    Measured 2026-09-01: read at the accepted plan's FIRST segment, this
    node holds through the TERMINAL CUSP of a plan it had already agreed
    to - the planner and the plan want the same thing and the decorator
    refuses both - and it turned two runs that arrive into two
    START_OCCUPIED aborts.

  CHILD FAILURE AND CHILD RUNNING PASS THROUGH UNTOUCHED, the 1 Hz replan
  is unchanged, and no plan is ever invented - the only path this node can
  write is one the planner produced and it had already accepted.

  hold_speed = 0.05 m/s IS config.yaml's (nav.direction_hold), REPEATED
  HERE because a BT port takes a literal. odom_topic is config.yaml's
  topics.odometry_filtered for the same reason. tests/test_bt_direction.py
  pins both copies to the file that owns them.

  THE OTHER THREE ATTRIBUTES ARE G5 TASK 6's AND THEY ARE THE SAME KIND
  OF COPY - config.yaml (nav.direction_hold) owns all three and the same
  test file pins them:
    hold_all         WHICH ARM. false refuses only a direction swap
                     (Task 5's node); true commits to the accepted plan
                     while the truck moves. THIS ATTRIBUTE IS WHAT THE
                     ACCEPTANCE CAMPAIGN SWITCHED, and the decorator
                     logs the mode it actually loaded at its first tick
                     so a result can be attributed to a process rather
                     than to a file.
    hold_max_s       the bound on the latch: an unbroken run of holds
                     older than this takes the fresh plan and logs `hold
                     expired`. Without it, commit mode IS the F1 pilot -
                     one plan, no correction, terminal overshoot.
    consume_floor_m  an accepted plan with less than this left in front
                     of the truck is a stub and is never held.
    ON THE --fuse ARM THE ESTIMATOR PUBLISHES SOMEWHERE ELSE
    (topics.fuse_odometry_filtered) and this attribute would name a topic
    nobody publishes. The decorator FAILS OPEN on that - it accepts every
    plan and says so once in bt_navigator's log - so that arm behaves
    exactly as it did before this node existed. Stated, not hidden.

  ---- THE NAVIGATION BUDGET, F4 TASK 2.5 ----

  `<Timeout msec="335000">` wraps the WHOLE tree, and it is the answer
  to EVIDENCE_NAV_V3.md 15.7 item 2: "a goal that cannot be reached
  should fail FAST, and nothing in this configuration makes it."
  Measured, before it existed: 130.199 m driven and 459 plans published
  for a goal 2.910 m away, and a second run that reached 479.7 s and was
  cancelled by the bench rather than failed by the stack.

  IT IS OUTSIDE THE TOP-LEVEL RecoveryNode AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT.
  BT.CPP's ReactiveSequence halts every non-running child on every tick
  ("reset the previous children, to make sure that they are in IDLE
  state the next time we tick them"), and nav2's own `<TimeExpired>`
  re-anchors its clock the moment it is halted - so the obvious
  construction, a ReactiveSequence with an inverted TimeExpired beside
  the pipeline, is a timer that can never expire. `<Timeout>` is a
  DECORATOR: it arms once when its child starts running, halts that
  child when the period elapses and returns FAILURE. Placed INSIDE the
  RecoveryNode it would be re-armed by each of the six retries and the
  bound would be seven times the number written; placed outside, the
  number written is the bound.

  WHEN IT FIRES THE TREE RETURNS FAILURE AND navigate_to_pose ABORTS.
  That is a nav2-side abort, so it protects any consumer of the action
  and not just tools/drive_goal.py.

  THE NUMBER IS DERIVED AND config.yaml OWNS THE DERIVATION.
  nav.budget: longest_route_m 45.709 (aisle_end's first plan, the road
  graph's own route), speed_fraction 0.5 (the route driven at half the
  0.300 m/s transit ceiling from end to end) and recovery_allowance_s
  30.0 (six retries of this tree's own Wait 5.0):

      ceil(45.709 / (0.300 * 0.5) + 30.0) = 335 s = 335000 ms

  tests/test_nav2_params.py recomputes it from config.yaml and
  nav2.yaml and fails if this attribute and that arithmetic disagree.

  TWO THINGS IT IS NOT.
    - It is a TIME bound and not a progress bound. It cannot tell a slow
      legitimate route from a vehicle orbiting its goal. The
      goal-relative guard is the bench's (config.yaml nav.watchdog), and
      the two are complementary by design: this one bounds ANY consumer
      crudely, that one names the actual failure precisely.
    - Its clock is BT.CPP's TimerQueue, which is std::chrono::steady -
      WALL time, where everything else on this track is the plant's.
      They are the real-time factor apart, and the RTF measured over
      these drives is 0.9963-1.0000 (EVIDENCE_NAV_V3.md 15.4, 16.5), so
      the budget is 335 s of wall clock and at worst 333.8 s of sim.
      That is recorded rather than hidden, and it is why the number is
      a bound with 2x of margin rather than a tight one.

  THIS FILE CONTAINS NO SAFETY LOGIC. Protective stop, e-stop and safe
  torque off are onboard and hardwired in the plant this models and
  appear in no behaviour tree. The collision monitor - which F4 Task 2
  does not run - "does not provide hard real-time safety
  certifications" and does not replace a safety-rated PLC. It
  complements the F-PLC; it is not the F-PLC.
-->
<root BTCPP_format="4" main_tree_to_execute="MainTree">
  <BehaviorTree ID="MainTree">
    <Timeout msec="335000" name="NavigationBudget">
      <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="6" name="NavigateRecovery">
        <PipelineSequence name="NavigateWithReplanning">
          <ControllerSelector selected_controller="{selected_controller}" default_controller="FollowPath" topic_name="controller_selector"/>
          <PlannerSelector selected_planner="{selected_planner}" default_planner="GridBased" topic_name="planner_selector"/>
          <RateController hz="1.0">
            <DirectionStablePath path="{path}" odom_topic="/m5v3/odometry/filtered" hold_speed="0.05" hold_all="false" hold_max_s="10.0" consume_floor_m="0.3">
              <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="1" name="ComputePathToPose">
                <ComputePathToPose goal="{goal}" path="{path}" planner_id="{selected_planner}" error_code_id="{compute_path_error_code}"/>
                <Sequence>
                  <WouldAPlannerRecoveryHelp error_code="{compute_path_error_code}"/>
                  <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearGlobalCostmap-Context" service_name="global_costmap/clear_entirely_global_costmap"/>
                </Sequence>
              </RecoveryNode>
            </DirectionStablePath>
          </RateController>
          <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="1" name="FollowPath">
            <FollowPath path="{path}" controller_id="{selected_controller}" error_code_id="{follow_path_error_code}"/>
            <Sequence>
              <WouldAControllerRecoveryHelp error_code="{follow_path_error_code}"/>
              <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearLocalCostmap-Context" service_name="local_costmap/clear_entirely_local_costmap"/>
            </Sequence>
          </RecoveryNode>
        </PipelineSequence>
        <Sequence>
          <Fallback>
            <WouldAControllerRecoveryHelp error_code="{follow_path_error_code}"/>
            <WouldAPlannerRecoveryHelp error_code="{compute_path_error_code}"/>
          </Fallback>
          <ReactiveFallback name="RecoveryFallback">
            <GoalUpdated/>
            <RoundRobin name="RecoveryActions">
              <Sequence name="ClearingActions">
                <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearLocalCostmap-Subtree" service_name="local_costmap/clear_entirely_local_costmap"/>
                <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearGlobalCostmap-Subtree" service_name="global_costmap/clear_entirely_global_costmap"/>
              </Sequence>
              <Wait wait_duration="5.0"/>
            </RoundRobin>
          </ReactiveFallback>
        </Sequence>
      </RecoveryNode>
    </Timeout>
  </BehaviorTree>
</root>
